### What is an LRU Cache?

An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache is a fixed-capacity key-value store. When the cache is full and a new entry arrives, it evicts the entry that was accessed least recently. The design that interviewers want makes both `get` and `put` run in O(1) time by combining a hash map with a doubly linked list.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class CacheNode {
    public String key;
    public String value;
    public CacheNode prev;
    public CacheNode next;

    public CacheNode(String key, String value) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
    }
}
```

### How do you implement an LRU Cache?

Use a `HashMap<String, CacheNode>` for O(1) lookups and a doubly linked list with sentinel head and tail for O(1) reordering. The list keeps the most recently used node right after the head sentinel and the least recently used node right before the tail sentinel.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.CacheNode;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<String, CacheNode> map;
    private final CacheNode head; // sentinel
    private final CacheNode tail; // sentinel

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.map = new HashMap<>();
        this.head = new CacheNode("", "");
        this.tail = new CacheNode("", "");
        head.next = tail;
        tail.prev = head;
    }

    public String get(String key) {
        CacheNode node = map.get(key);
        if (node == null) return null;
        moveToHead(node);
        return node.value;
    }

    public void put(String key, String value) {
        CacheNode existing = map.get(key);
        if (existing != null) {
            existing.value = value;
            moveToHead(existing);
        } else {
            CacheNode newNode = new CacheNode(key, value);
            map.put(key, newNode);
            addAfterHead(newNode);
            if (map.size() > capacity) {
                CacheNode lru = tail.prev;
                removeNode(lru);
                map.remove(lru.key); // must remove from map too!
            }
        }
    }

    private void addAfterHead(CacheNode node) {
        node.prev = head;
        node.next = head.next;
        head.next.prev = node;
        head.next = node;
    }

    private void removeNode(CacheNode node) {
        node.prev.next = node.next;
        node.next.prev = node.prev;
    }

    private void moveToHead(CacheNode node) {
        removeNode(node);
        addAfterHead(node);
    }
}
```

### How do get and put work step by step?

On `get(key)`: look up the node in the map. If absent, return null (cache miss). If present, move the node to the head (mark as most recently used) and return its value.

On `put(key, value)`: if the key exists, update its value and move it to the head. If the key is new, create a node, add it after the head, and put it in the map. If adding it exceeds capacity, remove the node just before the tail (least recently used) and remove its key from the map.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LRUCacheDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LRUCache cache = new LRUCache(3);
        cache.put("ACC-001", "Rajesh Savings");
        cache.put("ACC-002", "Priya Current");
        cache.put("ACC-003", "Joint Account");

        System.out.println(cache.get("ACC-001")); // moves ACC-001 to front

        cache.put("ACC-004", "Fixed Deposit"); // evicts ACC-002 (least recently used)

        System.out.println(cache.get("ACC-002")); // null (evicted)
        System.out.println(cache.get("ACC-003")); // still present
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh Savings
null
Joint Account
```

### Why are sentinel head and tail nodes important?

Sentinels eliminate null checks when inserting after the head or removing before the tail. Without sentinels, adding to an empty list or removing the last node requires special-case code. With sentinels, every node always has valid `prev` and `next` neighbors.

```java
// Without sentinels: special case for empty list
if (head == null) {
    head = newNode;
    tail = newNode;
} else {
    newNode.next = head;
    head.prev = newNode;
    head = newNode;
}

// With sentinels: uniform code, no null checks
node.prev = head;
node.next = head.next;
head.next.prev = node;
head.next = node;
```

### Why must you remove the evicted key from the map?

If you only remove the node from the list but forget to remove its key from the map, the map leaks memory and later lookups may find stale nodes. This is the most common bug in LRU cache implementations.

```java
// WRONG: only removes from list, map leaks
CacheNode lru = tail.prev;
removeNode(lru); // removed from list
// map still has the key! Memory leak and stale data!

// CORRECT: remove from both
CacheNode lru = tail.prev;
removeNode(lru);
map.remove(lru.key); // clean up the map entry
```

### What about Java's LinkedHashMap shortcut?

Java's `LinkedHashMap` with access order and an overridden `removeEldestEntry` provides a built-in LRU cache. However, building the map plus doubly linked list by hand demonstrates the mechanics interviewers want to see.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCacheShortcut extends LinkedHashMap<String, String> {
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCacheShortcut(int capacity) {
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true); // true = access order
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<String, String> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity;
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Capacity of 1 (every put evicts the previous entry), updating an existing key (should not trigger eviction), and getting a key that was already evicted.

```java
public static void edgeCases() {
    LRUCache cache = new LRUCache(1);
    cache.put("A", "1");
    cache.put("B", "2"); // evicts A
    System.out.println(cache.get("A")); // null
    System.out.println(cache.get("B")); // "2"

    // Update existing key should not evict
    cache.put("B", "3");
    System.out.println(cache.get("B")); // "3"
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand that the LRU cache is a building block for application-level caching, connection pooling, and page replacement in operating systems. Thread safety requires synchronization: either a `synchronized` wrapper or a `ReentrantReadWriteLock` for concurrent reads.

In production at National Bank of India, the account service caches hot account balances in an LRU cache. Promoting an account on every access means a frequently checked account is never evicted by a burst of unrelated lookups, keeping the cache hit rate high and database load low.

```java
// Thread-safe wrapper for production use
public class ConcurrentLRUCache {
    private final LRUCache cache;

    public ConcurrentLRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.cache = new LRUCache(capacity);
    }

    public synchronized String get(String key) {
        return cache.get(key);
    }

    public synchronized void put(String key, String value) {
        cache.put(key, value);
    }
}
```
